{% set guidance %}
This is progressive assay-transfer reasoning for absolute oral bioavailability
at the F=20% threshold. The target is measured absolute oral bioavailability,
not generic permeability, exposure, or drug-likeness.

Record cards combine a normalized measurement core with selected source-native
experimental context. Missing fields are omitted. First determine what each
measurement says about its reference molecule. Interpret absolute or relative
bioavailability and matched oral-versus-reference exposure using dose, route,
formulation, species, AUC, Cmax, sampling, and reference context together.
Permeability, solubility, exposure, metabolism, or transporter mechanisms alone
are indirect and cannot define the F >= 20% label.

L1 records define the accepted training context. L2 records did not define that
gold outcome: use them to clarify consistency, limitations, or transfer, never
as extra gold votes. Repeated records within one context are not independent.

Treat transfer_likelihood as one relevance signal for whether a reference
parent-condition context applies to the query. It is not a positive-class
probability or an experimental result. Infer direction from the records first,
then judge transfer from the visible endpoint, assay, species, formulation,
exposure, condition, and structure. A concrete mismatch can outweigh the score.
Do not invent a numeric similarity judgment or universal transfer cutoff.

{% if prompt_version in ("v8.1", "v8.2") %}
Make the final comparison at the parent-condition context level. Assign each
context one overall direction and applicability assessment, then compare the
most direct and transferable contexts rather than counting record cards. Use
the query physicochemical prior only when the transferable experimental
contexts are genuinely balanced or sparse; it must not override a coherent
set of directly measured outcomes.
{% endif %}

Missing or ambiguous evidence is neutral. A measured F < 20% outcome in an
applicable context is affirmative evidence for low bioavailability, while a
measured F >= 20% outcome is affirmative evidence for high bioavailability.
Ground every compound-specific empirical claim in cited record cards. Return
exactly one valid JSON object and do not reveal hidden chain-of-thought.
{% endset %}
[
  {
    "role": "system",
    "content": {{ (system_role ~ "\n\n" ~ (guidance | trim)) | tojson }}
  },
  {
    "role": "user",
    "content": {{ user_content | tojson }}
  }
]
